For every prime $p\geq5$, the canonical map $$\label{eq:main-map}
 \tau_{L_{K(3)}S}:L_0L_{K(3)}S\longrightarrow
                         L_0L_{K(2)}L_{K(3)}S$$ is nonzero on $\pi_{-3}$.

The actual maps of constants induce equivalences $$\begin{aligned}
 k&\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }R\Gamma(P_3^1,\widehat L),
       \label{eq:finite-constant-map}\\
 C^{\flat}&\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
       R\Gamma_v(X_C,\mathcal O^{\flat}).
       \label{eq:geometric-constant-map}
\end{aligned}$$ These are equivalences of derived solid algebras with residual $H$-action. The $H$-action on the left sides is trivial. The maps are natural for the change of constants $k\to C^{\flat}$ and for the arithmetic actions preserving the chosen base data.

There are continuous $G$-equivariant maps of solid $E_\infty$-rings $$\label{eq:completed-map}
 E_3^{\square}\longrightarrow\widehat B^{\square}
       \longleftarrow E_2^{\square},$$ where $G$ acts through $P_3$ on the first source and through $P_2$ on the second. The right arrow is canonical connected base change. The target is even periodic, with $$\pi_0\widehat B^{\square}(*)\simeq W(\widehat L)[[u'_1]].$$ Invariantly, its coefficients are complete base changes of the height-two deformation ring and its invertible even coefficient lines. The natural map $W(\widehat L)\to\pi_0\widehat B^{\square}$ is $G$-equivariant. Every profinite evaluation $\widehat B^{\square}(T)$ is an ordinary $K(2)$-local spectrum.*


*The second map in <a href="#eq:completed-map" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:completed-map">[eq:completed-map]</a> induces an $H$-equivariant equivalence $$E_2^{\square}\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
       (\widehat B^{\square})^{hP_3^1},$$ where $\overline Z$ acts trivially on the source. Moreover, for every $t\in\mathbb Z$ it induces an equivalence on the individual coefficient complexes $$\pi_tE_2^{\square}\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
       R\Gamma(P_3^1,\pi_t\widehat B^{\square}).$$ In particular, for $s\ge0$ and all $t$ there are natural isomorphisms $$\label{eq:coefficient-descent}
 H^s(H,\pi_tE_2^{\square})
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
 H^s(G,\pi_t\widehat B^{\square}).$$ These comparisons preserve the coefficient maps from Witt constants.*

*Finally, put $\Delta=\mathop{\mathrm{Gal}}(k/\mathbb F_p)$ and $G_n=P_n\rtimes\Delta$. The ordinary stabilizer-descent identification is $$\bigl((E_n^{\square})^{hG_n}\bigr)(*)\simeq L_{K(n)}S,
 \qquad n=2,3,$$ compatibly with the canonical maps to $E_n$.

The upper modification $\mathcal E_\ell$ is semistable of slope $1/n$ if and only if $\ell$ lies in no proper $\mathbb Q_p$-rational subspace. Consequently its semistable locus is $$\Omega_C^{n-1}
 =\mathbb P_C^{n-1}\setminus
       \bigcup_{\substack{W\subset\mathbb Q_p^n\\ \dim W=n-1}}\mathbb P(W_C).$$ On this locus $\mathcal E_\ell$ is a form of $\mathcal E_n^0$.

The tautological quotient of $\mathcal E_2$ and its composite with $f$ give maps $m_n:\mathcal T\to\mathcal M_n$ and an exact sequence $$\label{eq:filtered-modification}
 0\longrightarrow\ker(f)\longrightarrow\mathcal V_3
       \longrightarrow\mathcal V_2\longrightarrow0 .$$ These are slope-zero bundles. Their corresponding rational local systems form an exact sequence of ranks $1,3,2$. With the induced determinant lattices this sequence is classified by a map $d:\mathcal T\to B_CD$, where $$D=\left\{
 \begin{pmatrix}a&v\\0&A\end{pmatrix}:
       a\in\mathbb Z_p^\times,\quad A\in J_2,\quad v\in\mathbb Q_p^{1\times2}
       \right\}
   \simeq\mathbb Q_p^2\rtimes(\mathbb Z_p^\times\times J_2).$$ The classifying maps of $\mathcal V_3$ and $\mathcal V_2$ are the composites of $d$ with the relative maps induced by the inclusion $\rho_3:D%
  \BeginAccSupp{method=hex,unicode,ActualText=21AA}%
  \hookrightarrow\EndAccSupp{}J_3$ and projection $\rho_2:D\to J_2$. The $P_n$-classifying map of $m_n$ is the composite $\mathcal T\xrightarrow{\pi}Y_C\to B_CP_n$ induced by $q_n:G\to P_n$.

Let $X$ be a small v-stack over $\mathop{\mathrm{Spd}}C$, and let $\mathcal W$ be a rank-$(r+1)$ vector bundle on its untilt, with linear v-descent. For $g:\mathbb P(\mathcal W)\to X$, the compatible classes $h_m=c_1(\mathcal O(1))\in H_v^2(\mathbb P(\mathcal W),(\mathbb Z/p^m)(1))$ give equivalences $$\label{eq:projective-finite}
 \lambda_m:
 \bigoplus_{j=0}^r(\mathbb Z/p^m)(-j)[-2j]
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } Rg_*(\mathbb Z/p^m).$$ Consequently $$\label{eq:projective-integral}
 \bigoplus_{j=0}^r
  R\!\varprojlim_m R\Gamma_v(X,(\mathbb Z/p^m)(-j))[-2j]
 \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }
  R\!\varprojlim_m R\Gamma_v(\mathbb P(\mathcal W),\mathbb Z/p^m).$$ The term $j=0$ is the original pullback. Thus pullback is split injective on $H^*_{\mathrm{int}}$ and on $H^*_{\mathrm b}$. These statements are equivariant for actions on the vector bundle, including arithmetic Galois actions.

For $n=2,3$, the pullbacks of $e_{P_n}$ and $e_{J_n}$ to $H^3_{\mathrm b}(\mathcal M_n)$ are nonzero. There is a scalar $\kappa_n\in\mathbb Q_p^\times$ such that $$\label{eq:primitive-comparison}
 e_{P_n}|_{\mathcal M_n}
        =\kappa_n\,e_{J_n}|_{\mathcal M_n}.$$

There is a scalar $a\in\mathbb Q_p^\times$ such that the classes from the two stabilizer factors satisfy $$\label{eq:geometric-transport}
 e_{P_3}|_{Y_C}=a\,e_{P_2}|_{Y_C}
       \quad\text{in }H^3_{\mathrm b}(Y_C).$$

For the nonzero scalar $a\in\mathbb Q_p^\times$ of <a href="#thm:geometric-transport" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:geometric-transport">18</a>, one has $$\label{eq:witt-relation}
 c_3^W=a\,c_2^W
       \quad\hbox{in }H^3(G,W(\widehat L))[1/p],$$ and hence $$\label{eq:deformation-relation}
 c_3=a\,c_2
       \quad\hbox{in }H^3(G,\pi_0\widehat B^{\square})[1/p].$$ Under <a href="#eq:coefficient-descent" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:coefficient-descent">[eq:coefficient-descent]</a>, the preimage of $c_2$ in $$H^3(H,\pi_0E_2^{\square})[1/p]$$ is obtained by inflating $e_{P_2}$ and then including constants.

There is a natural map of ordinary spectra $$\label{eq:ordinary-detector}
 g:L_{K(3)}S\longrightarrow\mathcal D.$$ The spectrum $\mathcal D$ is $K(2)$-local, so $g$ factors through the canonical localization unit: $$\label{eq:detector-factorization}
 L_{K(3)}S\xrightarrow{\eta}L_{K(2)}L_{K(3)}S
       \xrightarrow{\overline g}\mathcal D.$$
